[
    {
        "content": "<p>I was thinking about formalizing the statement of BSD and one thing which always throws me off is that the proof that the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span>-function of the elliptic curve is even <em>defined</em> in a nhd of 1 is thousands of pages long! The BSD conjecture (which says something about the behaviour of this L-function near 1) only makes sense once one has established this theorem, which is harder than FLT -- indeed the only proof known to me of this goes via modularity, which implies FLT. </p>\n<p>But thinking about the Formal Conjectures repo, one rather nice way of sidestepping this would be to add to the Formal Conjectures the hard theorem that every elliptic curve over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8556em;vertical-align:-0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Q</span></span></span></span> is modular, and then assume this conjecture and deduce that the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span>-function is defined in a nhd of 1 by analytically continuing the L-function of the modular form. My understanding is that analytically continuing the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span>-function of a modular form of level <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma_0(N)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Γ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is <strong>easier</strong> than meromorphically continuing the Riemann zeta function, which we already have. How close are we to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span>-functions of modular forms? <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"464700\">@David Ang</span> ?</p>",
        "id": 591365428,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1777379050
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think you should be asking <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"389019\">@Chris Birkbeck</span> instead. This is basically the functional equation for the Hecke L-function right? I don't know how much we have. I did talk to <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"253861\">@Thomas Browning</span> after last LLL about computing coefficients of the L-function using modular symbols.</p>",
        "id": 591367447,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1777379523
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31187\">#31187</a>?</p>",
        "id": 591367698,
        "sender_full_name": "Weiyi Wang",
        "timestamp": 1777379570
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes I was going to say there is this work by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"481963\">@David Loeffler</span> . I also have some WIP on proving strong multiplicity one, where I am also developing some L-function theory.</p>",
        "id": 591368483,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Birkbeck",
        "timestamp": 1777379735
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31187\">#31187</a> defines the L-function of a modular form of level 1. There is in principle no significant difficulty in generalizing to the higher-level case, I just haven't got around to thinking about the right way to formulate the theorem (since we don't have the notion of \"eigenform\" yet, still less \"pseudo-eigenvalue for the Atkin-Lehner operator\" which is a somewhat slippery concept when the nebentype is nontrivial).</p>",
        "id": 591369546,
        "sender_full_name": "David Loeffler",
        "timestamp": 1777380010
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also, the formal conjectures repo already has the modularity conjecture <a href=\"https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/blob/main/FormalConjectures/Wikipedia/ModularityConjecture.lean\">here</a> which I added a while back.</p>",
        "id": 591413512,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Birkbeck",
        "timestamp": 1777389485
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe you can use <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_and_Swinnerton-Dyer_conjecture#History\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_and_Swinnerton-Dyer_conjecture#History</a> to state BSD without involving L-functions:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></msub><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\prod_{p\\leq x}\\frac{\\#E(\\mathbb{F}_p)}{p}\\approx C\\log(x)^{\\mathrm{rank}_{\\mathbb{Z}}E(\\mathbb{Q})}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.5134em;vertical-align:-0.4811em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mop op-symbol small-op\" style=\"position:relative;top:0em;\">∏</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1455em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4003em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span><span class=\"mrel mtight\">≤</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">x</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.4358em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.0323em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.655em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.5073em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">#</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mopen mtight\">(</span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathbb mtight\">F</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1645em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.357em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2819em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose mtight\">)</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.4811em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.138em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">lo<span style=\"margin-right:0.0139em;\">g</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.888em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathrm mtight\">rank</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.3527em;margin-right:0.0714em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.5em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathbb mtight\">Z</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1473em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mopen mtight\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathbb mtight\">Q</span><span class=\"mclose mtight\">)</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\to\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∞</span></span></span></span></p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 591427815,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1777392841
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"481963\">David Loeffler</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/L-function.20of.20a.20modular.20form/near/591369546\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31187\">#31187</a> defines the L-function of a modular form of level 1. There is in principle no significant difficulty in generalizing to the higher-level case, I just haven't got around to thinking about the right way to formulate the theorem (since we don't have the notion of \"eigenform\" yet, still less \"pseudo-eigenvalue for the Atkin-Lehner operator\" which is a somewhat slippery concept when the nebentype is nontrivial).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Maybe I'm missing something obvious; why can't you just define the Hecke integral, regardless of the weight? That will already be entire. Then you'll indeed need Atkin-Lehner to prove the functional equation, but not to make the definition, right? Then I guess you'd want to show the trivial bound for Fourier coefficients, to be able to conclude that, for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Re s</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">ℜ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span></span></span></span> sufficiently large, the Hecke integral is a (completed) L-function?</p>",
        "id": 591448645,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Kontorovich",
        "timestamp": 1777398737
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"252920\">Alex Kontorovich</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/L-function.20of.20a.20modular.20form/near/591448645\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"481963\">David Loeffler</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/L-function.20of.20a.20modular.20form/near/591369546\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/31187\">#31187</a> defines the L-function of a modular form of level 1. There is in principle no significant difficulty in generalizing to the higher-level case, I just haven't got around to thinking about the right way to formulate the theorem (since we don't have the notion of \"eigenform\" yet, still less \"pseudo-eigenvalue for the Atkin-Lehner operator\" which is a somewhat slippery concept when the nebentype is nontrivial).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Maybe I'm missing something obvious; why can't you just define the Hecke integral, regardless of the weight? That will already be entire. Then you'll indeed need Atkin-Lehner to prove the functional equation, but not to make the definition, right? Then I guess you'd want to show the trivial bound for Fourier coefficients, to be able to conclude that, for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℜ</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Re s</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">ℜ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span></span></span></span> sufficiently large, the Hecke integral is a (completed) L-function?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The machinery we have for analytic continuation of L-functions (the <code>FEPair</code> stuff) handles both analytic continuation + functional equation at the same time; and it expects as input a pair of functions f, g with f(1/x) = const * g x. (The trivial bound on Fourier coefficients is already done, btw.)</p>",
        "id": 591452275,
        "sender_full_name": "David Loeffler",
        "timestamp": 1777399689
    }
]